{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba p1a-mt/witnesses/w3mt-main.c | sed -n '1,295p'; nl -ba p1a-mt/witnesses/w3mt-server.scm; nl -ba p1a-mt/witnesses/Makefile | sed -n '1,210p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t/* W3MT fixture — foreign-pthread entry into Scheme on the MULTITHREADED-VM\n     2\t * 4.9.8 build (codex review step 4 experiment).\n     3\t *\n     4\t * Build (the manual's documented \"Scheme server\" pattern):\n     5\t *   gsc -exe -cc-options \"-D___LIBRARY\" -o w3mt.exe w3mt-main.c w3mt-server.scm\n     6\t *\n     7\t * Explicit parallelism (review step 2 requirement, applied to the server\n     8\t * pattern): the driver sets setup_params.parallelism_level = 2 — the\n     9\t * library-program equivalent of the gsi/gsc driver's -:p2 (main.c:46-49\n    10\t * parses -:p into setup_params.parallelism_level; setup.c consumes it).\n    11\t * GC-report verbosity (-:d5 equivalent) is set via debug_settings = 85\n    12\t * (level 5 | uncaught-all | error-quit), so real collections print the\n    13\t * runtime's own \"*** GC:\" reports.\n    14\t *\n    15\t * W3MT does NOT reuse the P1a W3 thunk-resize trick; the runtime's parallelism\n    16\t * comes from normal startup (___setup), never (##current-vm-resize ...).\n    17\t *\n    18\t * Phases (each run in its own process, under the external watchdog, so a\n    19\t * crash in one phase cannot erase the evidence of another):\n    20\t *\n    21\t *   owner    CONTROL (review F1 correction): the OS thread that ran ___setup\n    22\t *            — whose TLS is bound to processor 0 by ___setup itself\n    23\t *            (setup.c:6383-6406) — round-trips C -> Scheme -> C through the\n    24\t *            SAME entry points used later by the foreign pthread:\n    25\t *            scheme_w3mt_add (trivial), scheme_w3mt_add_alloc (allocates),\n    26\t *            scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds (real collections). This control must pass\n    27\t *            FIRST; the (int int) signatures are verified against the\n    28\t *            generated C (results/w3mt-generated-signatures.txt).\n    29\t *\n    30\t *   p1       serialized naive entry: raw pthread (never created by the\n    31\t *            runtime, never registered) calls scheme_w3mt_add while the\n    32\t *            owner thread parks in pthread_join. On this MT build the raw\n    33\t *            pthread's TLS pstate is NULL (gambit.h:8607-8612 vs 8645-8685:\n    34\t *            ___GET_REAL_PSTATE reads the thread-local ___tls_ptr, set only\n    35\t *            by ___thread_set_pstate — called exclusively at setup.c:6402\n    36\t *            for the primordial and setup.c:819 for runtime-created\n    37\t *            processors). Expected: crash on first Scheme entry.\n    38\t *\n    39\t *   p2       concurrent naive entry: raw pthread calls scheme_w3mt_add WHILE\n    40\t *            the owner thread runs Scheme (scheme_w3mt_spin allocates ~3s).\n    41\t *            Expected: crash/corruption (unregistered thread, NULL TLS).\n    42\t *\n    43\t *   bridge   BEST-EFFORT ATTACH BRIDGE (undocumented — every ingredient is\n    44\t *            cited; this is the review's demanded experiment: does the\n    45\t *            exported surface make foreign entry valid?):\n    46\t *            from the worker pthread, in order:\n    47\t *              1. read TLS pstate (expect NULL)              [TLS]\n    48\t *              2. vms = &___GSTATE->vmstate0; slot = vms->processor_count\n    49\t *                 (membership is the aligned_pstate[] ARRAY POSITION:\n    50\t *                 gambit.h:11966-11973 ___PROCESSOR_ID / 11934-11936;\n    51\t *                 ___setup_pstate does NOT touch processor_count —\n    52\t *                 setup.c:940-958 does that via OP_SET_PROCESSOR_COUNT\n    53\t *                 barrier_sync_op instead, setup.c:958-963)\n    54\t *              3. ___setup_pstate (ps, vms)                  [ownership:\n    55\t *                 exported gambit.h:14782-14784, but undocumented lifecycle;\n    56\t *                 initializes os+mem, processor object, registers, handlers,\n    57\t *                 catcher (setup.c:4675-4760)]                 [catcher]\n    58\t *              4. bind TLS: ___tls_ptr = ps (or ___set_tls_ptr, os_thread.c:\n    59\t *                 802-845 — ___USE_THREAD_LOCAL_STORAGE_GETTER_SETTER mode)  [TLS]\n    60\t *              5. scheme_w3mt_pid / add / add_alloc — first foreign entry\n    61\t *              6. scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds — real collections FROM the attached\n    62\t *                 pstate (membership stress: OP_GARBAGE_COLLECT barrier over\n    63\t *                 processor_count, setup.c:1182-1196 + 500-580, while this\n    64\t *                 processor's id >= processor_count)  [roots: interrupts are\n    65\t *                 raised ONLY for slots 0..processor_count-1 (setup.c:95-104)\n    66\t *                 and GC scan work-stealing covers only those pstates\n    67\t *                 (mem.c:6269-6280, 6678-6702) — the attached pstate is\n    68\t *                 invisible to every collection run by the other processors]\n    69\t *              7. teardown attempt: unbind TLS, ___cleanup_pstate\n    70\t *                 (gambit.h:14786-14787, setup.c:4665-4672)\n    71\t *            then a SECOND attach/detach round on the same slot (repeated\n    72\t *            attachment, same process).\n    73\t *\n    74\t *   bridge-concurrent  the bridge worker attaches and runs Scheme WHILE the\n    75\t *            owner thread allocates in Scheme (scheme_w3mt_spin) — the\n    76\t *            membership hole exercised concurrently: legit processors collect\n    77\t *            (their allocation pressure triggers OP_GARBAGE_COLLECT) without\n    78\t *            ever interrupting/scanning the attached mutator.\n    79\t *\n    80\t *   reattach standalone repeat-attachment run (attach -> call -> teardown ->\n    81\t *            attach again), only reached if bridge survived.\n    82\t *\n    83\t * Any attach sequence tried here is NOT a documented protocol: it mutates\n    84\t * runtime-internal state (VM pstate array slots, thread-local ___tls_ptr)\n    85\t * through exported-but-undocumented symbols. Membership is never established\n    86\t * (processor_count unchanged), so even a \"passing\" bridge cannot make GC-safe\n    87\t * foreign entry — that is the qualification criterion this phase probes.\n    88\t */\n    89\t\n    90\t#include <stdio.h>\n    91\t#include <stdlib.h>\n    92\t#include <string.h>\n    93\t#include <pthread.h>\n    94\t#include <unistd.h>\n    95\t\n    96\t#include <gambit.h>\n    97\t\n    98\t/* c-define'd in w3mt-server.scm — all two-argument procedures are declared\n    99\t   with two C parameters (review F1 correction) */\n   100\textern int scheme_w3mt_add (int x, int y);\n   101\textern int scheme_w3mt_add_alloc (int x, int y);\n   102\textern int scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds (int n);\n   103\textern int scheme_w3mt_pid (void);\n   104\textern int scheme_w3mt_spin (int ms);\n   105\t\n   106\t#define SCHEME_LIBRARY_LINKER ___LNK_w3mt_2d_server__\n   107\t___BEGIN_C_LINKAGE\n   108\textern ___mod_or_lnk SCHEME_LIBRARY_LINKER (___global_state);\n   109\t___END_C_LINKAGE\n   110\t\n   111\t#define W3MT_MAX_CSCORE 42   /* scheme_w3mt_add(20,22) */\n   112\t#define W3MT_MAX_ALLOCCSCORE 1072   /* 50+22+vector-length(make-vector 1000 50) */\n   113\t\n   114\tstatic void w3mt_log (const char *s)\n   115\t{\n   116\t  fputs (s, stdout);\n   117\t  fputc ('\\n', stdout);\n   118\t  fflush (stdout);\n   119\t}\n   120\t\n   121\tstatic void w3mt_log1 (const char *s, long v)\n   122\t{\n   123\t  printf (\"%s%ld\\n\", s, v);\n   124\t  fflush (stdout);\n   125\t}\n   126\t\n   127\t/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n   128\t/* workers                                                             */\n   129\t/* ------------------------------------------------------------------ */\n   130\t\n   131\tstatic pthread_t w3mt_tid;\n   132\t\n   133\tstatic void *w3mt_worker_naive (void *arg)\n   134\t{\n   135\t  int r;\n   136\t  (void) arg;\n   137\t  w3mt_log (\"W3MT-WORKER: raw pthread calling scheme_w3mt_add(20,22) — first foreign Scheme entry\");\n   138\t  r = scheme_w3mt_add (20, 22);\n   139\t  printf (\"W3MT-WORKER: scheme_w3mt_add(20,22) = %d (expected %d)\\n\",\n   140\t          r, W3MT_MAX_CSCORE);\n   141\t  fflush (stdout);\n   142\t  r = scheme_w3mt_add_alloc (50, 22);\n   143\t  printf (\"W3MT-WORKER: scheme_w3mt_add_alloc(50,22) = %d (expected %d)\\n\",\n   144\t          r, W3MT_MAX_ALLOCCSCORE);\n   145\t  fflush (stdout);\n   146\t  return (void *) (long) 0;\n   147\t}\n   148\t\n   149\tstatic void *w3mt_worker_concurrent (void *arg)\n   150\t{\n   151\t  int i, r;\n   152\t  long bad = 0;\n   153\t  (void) arg;\n   154\t  usleep (200000);   /* owner is definitely inside scheme_w3mt_spin by now */\n   155\t  for (i = 0; i < 10000; i++)\n   156\t    {\n   157\t      r = scheme_w3mt_add (1, 1);\n   158\t      if (r != 2)\n   159\t        bad++;\n   160\t    }\n   161\t  printf (\"W3MT-WORKER: completed 10000 concurrent entries, bad=%ld\\n\", bad);\n   162\t  fflush (stdout);\n   163\t  return (void *) 0;\n   164\t}\n   165\t\n   166\t/* ---- the best-effort attach bridge ---- */\n   167\t\n   168\tstatic int w3mt_bind_tls (___processor_state ps)\n   169\t{\n   170\t#ifdef ___USE_THREAD_LOCAL_STORAGE_GETTER_SETTER\n   171\t  ___set_tls_ptr (___CAST(void*,ps));\n   172\t#else\n   173\t  ___tls_ptr = ___CAST(void*,ps);\n   174\t#endif\n   175\t  return 0;\n   176\t}\n   177\t\n   178\tstatic int w3mt_unbind_tls (void)\n   179\t{\n   180\t#ifdef ___USE_THREAD_LOCAL_STORAGE_GETTER_SETTER\n   181\t  ___set_tls_ptr (0);\n   182\t#else\n   183\t  ___tls_ptr = 0;\n   184\t#endif\n   185\t  return 0;\n   186\t}\n   187\t\n   188\t/* one full attach -> enter -> (optional GC) -> teardown round.\n   189\t   keep_attached != 0 leaves the pstate attached (TLS bound, pstate live) —\n   190\t   the caller MUST tear down later.\n   191\t   returns 0 on full success, #failures otherwise (crash aborts everything). */\n   192\tstatic int w3mt_bridge_round (int round_no, int do_gc, int keep_attached)\n   193\t{\n   194\t  ___virtual_machine_state vms = &___GSTATE->vmstate0;\n   195\t  int n = vms->processor_count;\n   196\t  int slot = n;\n   197\t  ___processor_state ps;\n   198\t  ___SCMOBJ err;\n   199\t  int failures = 0;\n   200\t  int r, g;\n   201\t  long pid;\n   202\t\n   203\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: TLS pstate before attach = %p (raw pthread is unregistered: expected 0)\\n\",\n   204\t          round_no, ___CAST (void *, ___GET_REAL_PSTATE ()));\n   205\t  fflush (stdout);\n   206\t\n   207\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: vms->processor_count = %d, attaching at aligned_pstate slot %d (___MAX_PROCESSORS=%d)\\n\",\n   208\t          round_no, n, slot, (int) ___MAX_PROCESSORS);\n   209\t  fflush (stdout);\n   210\t\n   211\t  if (slot >= (int) ___MAX_PROCESSORS)\n   212\t    {\n   213\t      printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: slot beyond ___MAX_PROCESSORS — cannot attach\\n\",\n   214\t              round_no);\n   215\t      fflush (stdout);\n   216\t      return 1;\n   217\t    }\n   218\t\n   219\t  ps = ___PSTATE_FROM_PROCESSOR_ID (slot, vms);\n   220\t\n   221\t  err = ___setup_pstate (ps, vms);   /* exported (gambit.h:14782) but undocumented lifecycle */\n   222\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: ___setup_pstate(slot %d) = %ld (0 = no error)\\n\",\n   223\t          round_no, slot, (long) err);\n   224\t  fflush (stdout);\n   225\t  if (err != ___FIX(___NO_ERR))\n   226\t    return 1;\n   227\t\n   228\t  w3mt_bind_tls (ps);\n   229\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: TLS bound to attached pstate = %p (membership NOT established: vms->processor_count still %d)\\n\",\n   230\t          round_no, ___CAST (void *, ___GET_REAL_PSTATE ()), vms->processor_count);\n   231\t  fflush (stdout);\n   232\t\n   233\t  if (___GET_REAL_PSTATE () != ps)\n   234\t    {\n   235\t      printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: TLS binding FAILED\\n\", round_no);\n   236\t      fflush (stdout);\n   237\t      failures++;\n   238\t    }\n   239\t\n   240\t  /* first foreign Scheme entry through the attached pstate */\n   241\t  pid = scheme_w3mt_pid ();\n   242\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: first entry scheme_w3mt_pid() = %ld\\n\",\n   243\t          round_no, pid);\n   244\t  fflush (stdout);\n   245\t\n   246\t  r = scheme_w3mt_add (20, 22);\n   247\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: scheme_w3mt_add(20,22) = %d (expected %d) %s\\n\",\n   248\t          round_no, r, W3MT_MAX_CSCORE,\n   249\t          r == W3MT_MAX_CSCORE ? \"OK\" : \"WRONG\");\n   250\t  fflush (stdout);\n   251\t  if (r != W3MT_MAX_CSCORE)\n   252\t    failures++;\n   253\t\n   254\t  r = scheme_w3mt_add_alloc (50, 22);\n   255\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: scheme_w3mt_add_alloc(50,22) = %d (expected %d) %s\\n\",\n   256\t          round_no, r, W3MT_MAX_ALLOCCSCORE,\n   257\t          r == W3MT_MAX_ALLOCCSCORE ? \"OK\" : \"WRONG\");\n   258\t  fflush (stdout);\n   259\t  if (r != W3MT_MAX_ALLOCCSCORE)\n   260\t    failures++;\n   261\t\n   262\t  if (do_gc)\n   263\t    {\n   264\t      printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: requesting REAL collections from the attached pstate (membership stress: barrier over processor_count=%d)\\n\",\n   265\t              round_no, n);\n   266\t      fflush (stdout);\n   267\t      g = scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds (2);\n   268\t      printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds(2) returned %d (expected 2)\\n\",\n   269\t              round_no, g);\n   270\t      fflush (stdout);\n   271\t      if (g != 2)\n   272\t        failures++;\n   273\t    }\n   274\t\n   275\t  /* teardown attempt */\n   276\t  w3mt_unbind_tls ();\n   277\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: TLS unbound (TLS pstate now = %p)\\n\",\n   278\t          round_no, ___CAST (void *, ___GET_REAL_PSTATE ()));\n   279\t  fflush (stdout);\n   280\t\n   281\t  ___cleanup_pstate (ps);   /* exported (gambit.h:14786) but undocumented teardown */\n   282\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: round %d: ___cleanup_pstate(slot %d) returned\\n\",\n   283\t          round_no, slot);\n   284\t  fflush (stdout);\n   285\t\n   286\t  return failures;\n   287\t}\n   288\t\n   289\tstatic void *w3mt_worker_bridge (void *arg)\n   290\t{\n   291\t  int f1, f2;\n   292\t  (void) arg;\n   293\t  f1 = w3mt_bridge_round (1, 1, 0);\n   294\t  printf (\"W3MT-BRIDGE: attach round 1 failures = %d\\n\", f1);\n   295\t  fflush (stdout);\n     1\t;; W3MT server module — Scheme procedures called from C (c-define), plus the\n     2\t;; owner-thread allocation spinner.\n     3\t;;\n     4\t;; The codex review's F1 correction is applied: every c-define whose C callee\n     5\t;; passes two arguments declares TWO formals with (int int). Per the manual\n     6\t;; (doc/gambit.txi:19399-19412) the C argument types determine the arguments\n     7\t;; converted and passed to the Scheme procedure; the P1a W3 witnesses declared\n     8\t;; (int) while the C fixture called two-argument functions, which made their\n     9\t;; crashes reproduce a malformed entry fixture instead of isolating\n    10\t;; foreign-thread entry.\n    11\t;;\n    12\t;; The owner control (review step 4): BEFORE any foreign-pthread entry, the\n    13\t;; thread that ran ___setup must successfully round-trip C -> Scheme -> C\n    14\t;; through these same entry points (phase \"owner\" in w3mt-main.c).\n    15\t\n    16\t(c-define (scheme-w3mt-add x y) (int int) int \"scheme_w3mt_add\" \"extern\"\n    17\t  (+ x y))\n    18\t\n    19\t;; discriminator: same (int int) signature but the body ALLOCATES (same shape\n    20\t;; as W4-M4's callback) — isolates allocation-related failures from entry\n    21\t;; failures.\n    22\t(c-define (scheme-w3mt-add-alloc x y) (int int) int \"scheme_w3mt_add_alloc\" \"extern\"\n    23\t  (+ x y (vector-length (make-vector 1000 x))))\n    24\t\n    25\t;; real collections driven from whoever enters this procedure: allocate\n    26\t;; garbage, then force a REAL collection via the runtime primitive (##gc)\n    27\t;; (lib/_kernel.scm:2051-2069).\n    28\t(c-define (scheme-w3mt-gc-rounds n) (int) int \"scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds\" \"extern\"\n    29\t  (let loop ((i 1) (acc 0))\n    30\t    (if (<= i n)\n    31\t        (begin\n    32\t          (let ((v (make-vector 200000 0)))\n    33\t            (vector-set! v 0 i)\n    34\t            (##gc))\n    35\t          (loop (+ i 1) (+ acc 1)))\n    36\t        acc)))\n    37\t\n    38\t;; current processor id (SMP scheduler primitive, lib/_thread.scm:667) —\n    39\t;; lets the C driver record WHICH processor executed each entry.\n    40\t(c-define (scheme-w3mt-pid) () int \"scheme_w3mt_pid\" \"extern\"\n    41\t  (##current-processor-id))\n    42\t\n    43\t(c-declare \"void w3mt_spawn_worker_concurrent (void);\")\n    44\t\n    45\t(define w3mt-spawn-worker\n    46\t  (c-lambda () void \"w3mt_spawn_worker_concurrent (); ___return;\"))\n    47\t\n    48\t;; owner keeps the primordial processor INSIDE Scheme, allocating, for ~ms\n    49\t;; milliseconds after spawning the racing worker pthread (phase p2).\n    50\t(c-define (scheme-w3mt-spin ms) (int) int \"scheme_w3mt_spin\" \"extern\"\n    51\t  (begin\n    52\t    (w3mt-spawn-worker)\n    53\t    (let ((deadline (+ (time->seconds (current-time)) (/ ms 1000.0))))\n    54\t      (let loop ((allocs 0))\n    55\t        (if (< (time->seconds (current-time)) deadline)\n    56\t            (loop (+ allocs 1 (vector-length (make-vector 50000 0))))\n    57\t            allocs)))))\n     1\t# P1a-MT witnesses — multithreaded-4.9.8 experiment (jolt FFI track).\n     2\t#\n     3\t# The codex review's experiment (Q3 \"Experiment needed\", steps 1-5):\n     4\t#   1. pinned 4.9.8 build with --enable-multiple-threaded-vms --enable-smp\n     5\t#      --enable-max-processors=8, out-of-tree in ../gambit-builds/mt\n     6\t#   2. EXPLICIT parallelism: W0MT/W2MT via -:p2 on the command line (the\n     7\t#      gsi/gsc driver parses it, main.c:46-49); W3MT (server pattern) via\n     8\t#      setup_params.parallelism_level = 2 in w3mt-main.c\n     9\t#   3. W2MT blocking-GC witness with A/B synchronized before A enters C, B\n    10\t#      on a different processor, C released only after B's real GC rounds\n    11\t#      mutate live rooted objects, verdict from observed state, watchdogged\n    12\t#   4. W3MT foreign-pthread witness with (int int) signatures, owner-thread\n    13\t#      control first, then naive serialized/concurrent entry and the\n    14\t#      best-effort undocumented attach bridge (ingredients cited in\n    15\t#      w3mt-main.c), allocation/GC pressure, teardown, repeated attachment\n    16\t#   5. macOS arm64 now; Linux leg pending (follow-up, not run here)\n    17\t#\n    18\t# Every run is executed under ./watchdog.sh so a deadlock or crash is a\n    19\t# recorded FAILURE, never a hang. Outputs are recorded verbatim under\n    20\t# results/ with a \"### exit=\" line per run. Qualification is decided by the\n    21\t# witness verdicts (observed state), NOT by make's exit status.\n    22\t#\n    23\t# Toolchain: the MT build's OWN gsc (NOT the installed single-threaded one,\n    24\t# and NEVER bare `gsc` from PATH — that is Ghostscript). The build tree has no\n    25\t# bin/gsc (make install is broken out-of-tree), so gsc lives in gambit-builds/mt/gsc/\n    26\t# and is given the build tree's gambitdir explicitly via -: runtime options\n    27\t# (~~bin/~~lib/~~include), which gsc forwards to gambuild-C as\n    28\t# GAMBITDIR_INCLUDE/GAMBITDIR_LIB (_gsclib.scm ##gambuild, ##shell-install-dirs).\n    29\t# The compiler needs an explicit min-heap: on this MT build the stock default\n    30\t# heap fails to boot when the processor count makes the initial msection pool\n    31\t# insufficient (see results/mt-default-parallelism.out for the recorded\n    32\t# side-experiment), and heap GROWTH during a -:p2 gsc -exe of a multi-module\n    33\t# program segfaults deterministically inside the compiler (___H___gvm, data\n    34\t# abort writing near-NULL, processor 1 idle in the scheduler; recorded in\n    35\t# BUILD-CONFIG.md). A large initial heap avoids growth entirely.\n    36\tGSCBUILD ?= /Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-mt/p1a-mt/gambit-builds/mt\n    37\tGSCOPTS  ?= -:~~bin=$(GSCBUILD)/bin,~~lib=$(GSCBUILD)/lib,~~include=$(GSCBUILD)/include,min-heap=256M,max-heap=1G,p2\n    38\tGSC ?= $(GSCBUILD)/gsc/gsc $(GSCOPTS)\n    39\tSH  ?= ./watchdog.sh\n    40\tRES ?= results\n    41\t\n    42\t.PHONY: all clean build-witnesses record\n    43\t\n    44\tall: build-witnesses record\n    45\t\n    46\tbuild-witnesses: w0mt.exe w2mt.exe w3mt.exe\n    47\t\n    48\trecord: record-build-config record-w0mt record-w2mt record-w3mt\n    49\t\n    50\t$(RES):\n    51\t\tmkdir -p $(RES)\n    52\t\n    53\t# ---- build configuration record (review step 1) --------------------------\n    54\t\n    55\trecord-build-config: | $(RES)\n    56\t\t@echo \"### configure invocation (cwd=$(CURDIR)/../gambit-builds/mt, out-of-tree from /Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src):\" > $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    57\t\t@echo \"### /Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src/configure --enable-single-host --enable-multiple-threaded-vms --enable-smp --enable-max-processors=8\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    58\t\t@echo \"### compiler:\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    59\t\t@clang --version | head -2 >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1\n    60\t\t@echo \"### make: make -j8 (Apple clang 21.0.0, macOS 26.6.2 / Darwin 25.6.0, arm64, 8 CPUs)\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    61\t\t@echo \"### out-of-tree build fixes applied inside the build tree ONLY:\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    62\t\t@echo \"###   1. copied gsc-boot.unix + gsc-boot.bat.windows from the source root into the build root (dummy-gsc-boot rule uses bare relative paths)\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    63\t\t@echo \"###   2. rewrote GAMBITDIR_INCLUDE and the \\$(srcdirpfx)\\$(rootfromhere)/include dependency paths in generated makefiles to the build tree's include dir (configure-generated gambit.h lives there, not in the source tree)\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    64\t\t@echo \"### generated gambit.h threading macros (build tree):\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    65\t\t@grep -n \"___MULTIPLE_THREADED_VMS\\|___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS\\|___MAX_PROCESSORS\" ../gambit-builds/mt/include/gambit.h | head -8 >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1\n    66\t\t@echo \"### installed single-threaded provisioned gambit.h (mise) for comparison:\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    67\t\t@grep -n \"___SINGLE_THREADED_VMS\\|___MULTIPLE_THREADED_VMS\\|___MAX_PROCESSORS\" /Users/sto/.local/share/mise/installs/gambit/4.9.8/include/gambit.h | head -6 >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1\n    68\t\t@echo \"### source pin:\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    69\t\t@cd /Users/sto/workspace/gambit-v4.9.8-src && git rev-parse HEAD >> $(CURDIR)/$(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1 && git describe --tags >> $(CURDIR)/$(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1\n    70\t\t@echo \"### this worktree pin:\" >> $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    71\t\t@cd /Users/sto/workspace/jolt-ffi-mt && git rev-parse HEAD >> $(CURDIR)/$(RES)/build-configure.txt 2>&1\n    72\t\t@cat $(RES)/build-configure.txt\n    73\t\n    74\t# ---- builds -------------------------------------------------------------\n    75\t\n    76\tw0mt.exe: w0mt-config.scm\n    77\t\t$(GSC) -warnings -exe -o $@ $<\n    78\t\n    79\tw2mt.exe: w2mt.scm w2mt-fixture.c\n    80\t\t$(GSC) -warnings -exe -o $@ w2mt.scm w2mt-fixture.c\n    81\t\n    82\t# The manual's documented \"Scheme server\" build pattern\n    83\t# (doc/gambit.txi §c-define; tests/makefile.in pattern). -keep-temp retains\n    84\t# the generated C so the (int int) signature check can be recorded verbatim.\n    85\tw3mt.exe: w3mt-main.c w3mt-server.scm\n    86\t\t$(GSC) -warnings -keep-temp -exe -cc-options \"-D___LIBRARY\" -o $@ w3mt-main.c w3mt-server.scm\n    87\t\n    88\t# ---- recorded runs ------------------------------------------------------\n    89\t\n    90\trecord-w0mt: w0mt.exe | $(RES)\n    91\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 60 ./w0mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M\" > $(RES)/w0mt.out\n    92\t\t@$(SH) 60 ./w0mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M >> $(RES)/w0mt.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w0mt.out\n    93\t\t@cat $(RES)/w0mt.out\n    94\t\t@echo \"### side-record: stock DEFAULT parallelism (0 -> 50% of 8 CPUs = 4) on this MT build:\" > $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n    95\t\t@echo \"### cmd: ./w0mt.exe   (NO runtime options: default parallelism 4 processors)\" >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n    96\t\t@$(SH) 30 ./w0mt.exe >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n    97\t\t@echo \"### cmd: ./w0mt.exe -:p2  (EXPLICIT parallelism: works)\" >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n    98\t\t@$(SH) 30 ./w0mt.exe -:p2 >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n    99\t\t@cat $(RES)/mt-default-parallelism.out\n   100\t\n   101\trecord-w2mt: w2mt.exe | $(RES)\n   102\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 90 ./w2mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M control\" > $(RES)/w2mt-control.out\n   103\t\t@$(SH) 90 ./w2mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M control >> $(RES)/w2mt-control.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w2mt-control.out\n   104\t\t@cat $(RES)/w2mt-control.out\n   105\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 90 ./w2mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M blocked\" > $(RES)/w2mt-blocked.out\n   106\t\t@$(SH) 90 ./w2mt.exe -:p2,d5,min-heap=64M,max-heap=512M blocked >> $(RES)/w2mt-blocked.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w2mt-blocked.out\n   107\t\t@cat $(RES)/w2mt-blocked.out\n   108\t\n   109\trecord-w3mt: w3mt.exe | $(RES)\n   110\t\t@echo \"### generated C signature check (review F1 correction: two formals for (int int)):\" > $(RES)/w3mt-generated-signatures.txt\n   111\t\t@grep -n \"int scheme_w3mt_add\\|___NARGS\" w3mt-server.c w3mt-server_.c 2>/dev/null | head -16 >> $(RES)/w3mt-generated-signatures.txt 2>&1\n   112\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-generated-signatures.txt\n   113\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe owner\" > $(RES)/w3mt-owner.out\n   114\t\t@$(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe owner >> $(RES)/w3mt-owner.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-owner.out\n   115\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-owner.out\n   116\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe p1\" > $(RES)/w3mt-p1.out\n   117\t\t@$(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe p1 >> $(RES)/w3mt-p1.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-p1.out\n   118\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-p1.out\n   119\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe p2\" > $(RES)/w3mt-p2.out\n   120\t\t@$(SH) 60 ./w3mt.exe p2 >> $(RES)/w3mt-p2.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-p2.out\n   121\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-p2.out\n   122\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 120 ./w3mt.exe bridge\" > $(RES)/w3mt-bridge.out\n   123\t\t@$(SH) 120 ./w3mt.exe bridge >> $(RES)/w3mt-bridge.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-bridge.out\n   124\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-bridge.out\n   125\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 120 ./w3mt.exe bridge-concurrent\" > $(RES)/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out\n   126\t\t@$(SH) 120 ./w3mt.exe bridge-concurrent >> $(RES)/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out\n   127\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out\n   128\t\t@echo \"### cmd: $(SH) 180 ./w3mt.exe reattach\" > $(RES)/w3mt-reattach.out\n   129\t\t@$(SH) 180 ./w3mt.exe reattach >> $(RES)/w3mt-reattach.out 2>&1; echo \"### exit=$$?\" >> $(RES)/w3mt-reattach.out\n   130\t\t@cat $(RES)/w3mt-reattach.out\n   131\t\n   132\tclean:\n   133\t\trm -f *.exe *.o\n   134\t\trm -f w0mt-config.c w2mt.c w2mt_.c w3mt-server.c w3mt-server_.c\n   135\t\trm -rf $(RES)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
