 was captured for attribution in this revision.)\n   446\t\n   447\t**Witness W3 (foreign-thread entry, p1a-r2) — owner-thread control PASS;\n   448\tserialized owner-parked shapes survive (REFUSAL-NOT-REPRODUCED — 3/3 across\n   449\trepetitions for each shape, and in each recorded run); concurrent entry\n   742\t**VERDICT-AMENDED (p1a-r2, adopting the reviewer's amended verdict): NO-GO\n   743\tfor P2/P3 on the provisioned build CONFIRMED; a multithreaded 4.9.8\n   744\tconfiguration remains unqualified and does not clear that verdict; the\n   745\t\"foreign-entry-impossible\" claim is WITHDRAWN; PARTIAL-GO permits bounded\n   746\tP1b work only as each capability is independently qualified.** Applying\n   747\tastra's exact wording:\n   748\t\n   749\t- \"**NO-GO for P2/P3:** collection cannot complete while C is blocked\" —\n   750\t  ESTABLISHED by the repaired W2 on the pinned runtime: the qualify witness\n   751\t  synchronizes both participants before the block, B is proven executing,\n   752\t  the release handshake IS called from Scheme after B's real collections,\n   753\t  and the OBSERVED state is still `release-cause=deadline`,\n   754\t  `rounds-during-block=0`, `handshake-during-block=0` over the 6 s blocked\n   755\t  window. This criterion alone forces NO-GO for P2/P3, because \"P2 remains\n   756\t  Chez-only if P1 blocking is unqualified\" and \"Removing `:blocking` from\n   757\t  crypto calls is not an acceptable workaround\".\n   758\t- \"**unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required**\" — the\n   759\t  P1a claim that W3 PROVED this is WITHDRAWN (review finding F1: the P1a\n   760\t  fixture exported one argument to two-formal procedures, so its 3/3 crashes\n   761\t  reproduced a malformed entry fixture). The refusal row instead rests on:\n   762\t  no documented foreign-pthread attach protocol for any 4.9.8 configuration\n   763\t  (Q4), no release/claim mechanism (Q2, configuration-scoped), and the\n   764\t  repaired W3's fresh evidence — concurrent foreign-thread entry while the\n   765\t  owner runs Scheme crashes with fatal signals (139/138, valid fixture,\n   766\t  3/3), while serialized owner-parked survival is an undocumented artifact\n   767\t  that nothing makes legitimate (no rooted context, no membership, no TLS\n   768\t  binding). That supports NO-GO without the withdrawn inference.\n   769\t- \"**exact runtime ownership rules remain unknown**\" — the provisioned\n   770\t  build's rules are known (one pstate, one OS thread, no release/claim, no\n   771\t  foreign-thread protocol), which closes the audit for that configuration;\n   772\t  the multiple-threaded-VMs/SMP variants carry materially different TLS,\n   773\t  startup, scheduling and barrier paths (Q2 amendment) that remain\n   774\t  UNQUALIFIED — configuration-only adoption does not close the gate (review\n   775\t  Q3/Q4).\n   776\t- The worker-pool escape is pre-ruled by astra (\"A worker pool is **not** a\n   777\t  transparent generic fallback ... cannot masquerade as unrestricted\n   778\t  `sa-foreign-procedure-blocking`\"). The p1a-r2 evidence sharpens it: a\n   779\t  foreign pthread CAN survive serialized owner-parked reuse (W3 phase1/3,\n   780\t  W4-M4 — artifact), but there is no protocol making that legitimate, and\n   781\t  concurrent use crashes — so a pool would still have to run calls on the\n   782\t  SAME (only) Scheme-executing OS thread, which is precisely the\n   783\t  serialization that defeats blocking.\n   784\t- **Partial GO (astra's fallback ruling):** \"memory, loading, ordinary calls,\n   785\t  and errno may land independently when qualified.\" W1 (errno, repaired:\n   786\t  capture-before-allocation with negative controls) PASSes; the ordinary-call\n   787\t  tier is exercised throughout W1/W2-control/W3-control/W4-M1/M2 via\n   788\t  c-lambda/c-define with correct results; the memory/loader tier was not\n   789\t  separately qualified here (dlopen/sa-foreign-alloc are NOT part of P1a's\n   790\t  witness list — they are P1b implementation targets; nothing observed\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-e0eNFYTB' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-19 01:57:21.768 xcodebuild[80502:141807452]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-19 01:57:22.028 xcodebuild[80502:141807449] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-DflT9f2y' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-11-19 01:57:22.883 xcodebuild[80506:141807477]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-11-19 01:57:23.134 xcodebuild[80506:141807476] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"agent_message","text":"The MT blocked-GC failure reproduces in my rerun. I also found a defect in the attach stress test: `keep_attached` is ignored, so the helper clears TLS and frees the pstate before the caller’s “while attached” loop enters Scheme. That crash does not establish teardown corruption. The recorded GC timeout also occurs with the owner parked in `pthread_join`, so it cannot isolate the membership defect from the blocked-owner barrier. These limits do not change the blocking NO-GO."}}
