{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"ls -l /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/git; nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/jolt/1795034000000-engineer-p1a-mt-experiment.glm-5.3-flash.findings.md | sed -n '145,285p'; nl -ba p1a/witnesses/build/results/w2-qualify.out | tail -8; nl -ba p1a/witnesses/build/results/w1.out; nl -ba p1a/witnesses/build/w3-server.c | sed -n '195,205p;223,233p'\"","aggregated_output":"-rwxr-xr-x  1 root  wheel  3838240 Oct 20 09:01 /Applications/Xcode.app/Contents/Developer/usr/bin/git\n   145\t(allocation/round-setup on the free processor) but the collection barrier\n   146\t(setup.c:1182-1196 `___garbage_collect` → `on_all_processors` → tree barrier\n   147\tover `___vms->processor_count`, setup.c:500-580) waits for the processor\n   148\tparked inside the foreign call, freezing the whole VM until C returns.\n   149\t\n   150\t**Release/claim bridge: NONE implemented — and none exists to implement.**\n   151\tSearches over the stock 4.9.8 source and headers for release/claim/suspend/\n   152\tdetach/absence/leave/reclaim mechanisms found nothing: the FFI macros only\n   153\tsave/restore Scheme registers around C execution (gambit.h:10310-10334); no\n   154\tprocessor is ever marked absent from GC participation; `OP_GARBAGE_COLLECT`\n   155\tis `COMBINING_ADD` over every processor (`processor_count` participants).\n   156\tAbsence of a mechanism is itself the finding (review step 3).\n   157\t\n   158\t## Step 4 — foreign-pthread witness with correct signatures (W3MT)\n   159\t\n   160\tSource `p1a-mt/witnesses/w3mt-main.c` + `w3mt-server.scm` (manual server\n   161\tpattern build, records `witnesses/results/w3mt-*.out`).\n   162\t\n   163\t**F1 correction verified in generated C:** `results/w3mt-generated-signatures.txt`\n   164\tshows `scheme_w3mt_add ___P((int ___arg1,int ___arg2)` with `___NARGS 2` (and\n   165\tsame for `scheme_w3mt_add_alloc`) — the two-formal defect of the P1a W3 is\n   166\tfixed; runtime values confirm (add(20,22)=42, add_alloc(50,22)=1072).\n   167\t\n   168\t**Owner-thread control (review: \"establish a successful owner-thread control\n   169\tbefore comparing\"): PASS** (`results/w3mt-owner.out`). The OS thread that ran\n   170\t`___setup` (whose TLS is bound to processor 0 by `___setup` itself,\n   171\tsetup.c:6383-6406) round-trips C→Scheme→C through the same entry points used\n   172\tlater by the foreign pthread: trivial add, allocating add, and 2 real GC\n   173\trounds — all correct. The documented server pattern WORKS on the MT+SMP build\n   174\tfrom the owner thread (this is new, positive evidence vs the single-threaded\n   175\tbuild's heartbeat-sensitive phase1).\n   176\t\n   177\t**Naive foreign-pthread entry (no registration): SIGSEGV, exit 139, in BOTH\n   178\tserialized and concurrent modes** (`results/w3mt-p1.out`, `w3mt-p2.out`). The\n   179\traw pthread's TLS pstate is NULL (`___GET_REAL_PSTATE()` reads thread-local\n   180\t`___tls_ptr`, bound only by `___thread_set_pstate` at setup.c:6402 for the\n   181\tprimordial and setup.c:819 for runtime-created processors) — first Scheme\n   182\tentry faults. On this MT build the crash is IMMEDIATE and deterministic\n   183\t(different signature from the single-threaded build's heartbeat-dependent\n   184\tserialized reuse), and it is not caused by my P1a signature defect (F1) —\n   185\tthe entry is still invalid without registration.\n   186\t\n   187\t**Best-effort attach bridge (review step 4: \"only if you implement a\n   188\trelease/claim bridge — that bridge with its exact implementation cited\";\n   189\there the closest analog — the attach bridge assembled from the\n   190\texported-but-undocumented surface — WAS implemented and is cited exactly):\n   191\tentry survives; GC and teardown do not** (`results/w3mt-bridge.out`,\n   192\t`w3mt-bridge-concurrent.out`, `w3mt-reattach.out`). Exact sequence, from the\n   193\tworker pthread (all six ingredients documented, cited, and observed):\n   194\t\n   195\t- membership: never established. `vms->processor_count` is read (2) and the\n   196\t  pstate is initialized at array slot `aligned_pstate[processor_count]` = 2\n   197\t  (identity IS array position, gambit.h:11966-11973,11934-11936) — but\n   198\t  `processor_count` is NOT updated (`___setup_pstate` does not touch it;\n   199\t  membership is only changed via the OP_SET_PROCESSOR_COUNT barrier,\n   200\t  setup.c:940-963) — the attached processor is invisible to every\n   201\t  VM-wide operation (`___raise_interrupt_vmstate` iterates slots\n   202\t  `0..processor_count-1`, setup.c:95-104; GC scan work-stealing covers the\n   203\t  same range, mem.c:6269-6280, 6678-6702).\n   204\t- ownership: the pstate storage is the VM's own pre-allocated\n   205\t  `aligned_pstate[]` slot; its initialization is a direct call to the\n   206\t  EXPORTED but UNDOCUMENTED `___setup_pstate` (gambit.h:14782; sets up\n   207\t  os+mem, the Scheme processor object, registers, handlers, catcher —\n   208\t  setup.c:4675-4760). No API contract; runtime-internal machinery invoked by\n   209\t  foreign code.\n   210\t- TLS: bound via `___tls_ptr = ps` (direct TLS class mode on this build;\n   211\t  `___set_tls_ptr` in getter/setter mode) — observed bound value printed.\n   212\t- catcher: handlers copied by `___setup_pstate` (handler_return_to_c etc.),\n   213\t  catcher=0; the foreign thread's REAL C stack is never registered — the\n   214\t  Scheme stack used is the pstate's own allocated stack.\n   215\t- roots: none for the attached processor — its stack/heap are NOT scanned by\n   216\t  collections run by the other processors (citations above), and it never\n   217\t  receives interrupts, so it also never stops mutating during collection.\n   218\t- teardown: `___tls_ptr = 0` + `___cleanup_pstate` (gambit.h:14786;\n   219\t  setup.c:4665-4672) — returns, but the next Scheme entry after teardown\n   220\t  SIGSEGVs and the process dies shortly after cleanup with the owner still\n   221\t  allocating (observed in bridge-concurrent) — no detach protocol exists.\n   222\t\n   223\tObserved bridge results: `___setup_pstate` returns 0; TLS binds; FIRST\n   224\tScheme entry on the attached pstate EXECUTES (pid=2 — the array-slot id);\n   225\t`scheme_w3mt_add(20,22)`=42 and the allocating `add_alloc`=1072 return\n   226\tcorrect values. Then REAL collections FROM the attached pstate\n   227\t(`scheme_w3mt_gc_rounds`) HANG THE ENTIRE VM — watchdog SIGKILL at 120s/180s\n   228\tin both `bridge` and `reattach` (repeated attachment never reached: cycle 1\n   229\thangs). With an initiator id >= `processor_count`, the tree barrier\n   230\t(setup.c:500-580) has undefined structure: deadlock is the observed\n   231\tresolution. This is precisely the review's predicted membership hole,\n   232\tdemonstrated live.\n   233\t\n   234\t**Qualification: FAIL.** Even the best case (undocumented bridge, entries\n   235\treturning correct values) is not a documented protocol, does not establish\n   236\tGC membership, deadlocks on collection, and corrupts on teardown. The review's\n   237\t(c) stands with richer evidence.\n   238\t\n   239\t## Step 5 — platform\n   240\t\n   241\tmacOS arm64 executed (this record). Linux x86_64 leg: PENDING — follow-up,\n   242\tnot run here (no ssh per brief).\n   243\t\n   244\t## Side findings (recorded verbatim, tangential to the verdict)\n   245\t\n   246\t1. Stock DEFAULT parallelism (0 → 50% of 8 CPUs = 4) fails to boot on this\n   247\t   platform: `*** FATAL ERROR -- Heap overflow`, exit 70 — recorded in\n   248\t   `results/mt-default-parallelism.out` for both gsi and compiled programs.\n   249\t   Only explicit smaller parallelism (or explicit heap) boots. The review's\n   250\t   insistence on explicit `-:p` is also a correctness requirement here.\n   251\t2. Heap GROWTH under -:p2 crashed the compiler deterministically during\n   252\t   `gsc -exe` of a multi-module program (SIGSEGV in `___H___gvm`, data abort\n   253\t   writing near-NULL far=0x51 on the primordial thread while processor 1\n   254\t   idles in the scheduler; crash report preserved at\n   255\t   `p1a-mt/gambit-builds/logs/gsc-crash-report.ips`). A large initial\n   256\t   min-heap (256M for the compiler, 64M for witnesses) avoids growth entirely\n   257\t   and the crash disappears. This suggests the MT build's heap-growth path\n   258\t   (msection pool growth with multiple processors) is fragile on macOS arm64.\n   259\t3. The intermediate NON-SMP-scheduler run (same configure, prebuilt lib .c)\n   260\t   is preserved in `witnesses/results-nonsmp/` — with multiple-threaded VMS\n   261\t   but the cooperative scheduler, thread B was never scheduled at all during\n   262\t   the block (both threads' pids = 0). Consistent with the review's warning\n   263\t   that \"Multiple-threaded VMs alone must not be equated with parallel\n   264\t   scheduling\".\n   265\t\n   266\t## Q4-style verdict sanity\n   267\t\n   268\tThe one-processor starvation was already a configuration property; this\n   269\texperiment shows the SAME structural obstacle (all-processor GC barrier +\n   270\tno absence marking + no documented attach protocol) in the explicitly\n   271\tSMP-scheduled multithreaded configuration, now with executed evidence\n   272\tinstead of source reading alone. Per the review's NO-GO criteria:\n   273\t\"collection cannot complete while C is blocked\" — CONFIRMED on the MT build;\n   274\t\"unrelated C-thread entry cannot be made safe where required\" — confirmed\n   275\t(naive entry crashes; the only working entry shape is an undocumented,\n   276\tmembership-less bridge that deadlocks on GC and corrupts on teardown).\n   277\t\n   278\tMT-VERDICT: MT build fails: on macOS arm64 with --enable-single-host\n   279\t--enable-multiple-threaded-vms --enable-smp --enable-max-processors=8, -:p2,\n   280\tSMP scheduler verified live (threads on processors 0 and 1), W2MT blocked\n   281\tmode FAILED qualification — A blocked 6.005s in an ordinary c-lambda on\n   282\tprocessor 1, B executed on processor 0, B's (##gc) request waited the full\n   283\tblock (collection completed 183us after C's deadline return; C returned by\n   284\tdeadline, never by handshake; live rooted objects only intact post-return);\n   285\tno release/claim bridge exists in the stock source; W3MT owner control PASSED\n    18\tW2-B: round 3 t=1795028149.003713 start (c-in-block=0 a-calls-done=0)\n    19\t*** GC: 488us, 162K alloc, 6.5M heap, 1.6M live (24% 68784+1607864)\n    20\tW2-B: round 3 t=1795028149.005019 done (capi-gc-return=0)\n    21\tW2-A: foreign call returned t=1795028149.005075 release-cause=deadline\n    22\tW2: observed state: release-cause=deadline rounds-during-block=0 rounds-after=3 handshake-called-from-scheme=#t capi-handshake-calls=1 handshake-during-block=0 blocked-ms=6000 witness-object=(last-round=3 round-count=3)\n    23\tW2-QUALIFY-VERDICT: FAIL (blocking unqualified on this build: release-cause=deadline, rounds-during-block=0, handshake-during-block=0; B was synchronized and provably executing BEFORE A entered C, but completed all rounds only AFTER the foreign call returned — the handshake was called from Scheme only after B's real collections, which on this build can only happen after C returned; astra §2 'actual collection and another Scheme thread's progress while C remains blocked' is not satisfied)\n    24\t### exit=0\n    25\t### w2-outcome: run completed (verdict derived from observed state; see the W2-QUALIFY-VERDICT line)\n     1\t### cmd: ./watchdog.sh 120 ./w1.exe\n     2\t=== W1 errno-atomicity witness (p1a-r2: capture-before-allocation + negative controls) ===\n     3\tEAGAIN=35 EACCES=13 EIO=5 intervening-clobber=4242\n     4\tW1-NEGATIVE-CONTROL: OK (shape G captured AFTER the intervening clobber and reported it — the assertions distinguish capture order, so the immediate-capture shapes are load-bearing)\n     5\tW1-CHECKS=5901\n     6\tW1-FAILURES=0\n     7\tW1-VERDICT: PASS (every shim captured (result,errno) immediately after the target, before ANY allocation/conversion/re-entry: shapes A/C preserve the target's own errno, shape B preserves errno=42 on success, shape E captures before the nested Scheme re-entry, shape F defeats an intervening clobber written between target and capture; shape G proves the assertions distinguish capture order; the ___AT_END clobber (shape D) and the two-thread interleave add conversion-boundary and scheduling pressure)\n     8\t### exit=0\n   195\t#define ___C_LBL_scheme_w3_phase3 74\n   196\t\n   197\textern int scheme_w3_add ___P((int ___arg1,int ___arg2),(___arg1,___arg2)\n   198\tint ___arg1;\n   199\tint ___arg2;)\n   200\t{\n   201\t#define ___NARGS 2\n   202\t___BEGIN_SFUN(___MLBL(___C_LBL_scheme_w3_add),int ___result)\n   203\t___BEGIN_SFUN_ARG(1,___ARG1)\n   204\t___BEGIN_SFUN_INT_TO_SCMOBJ(___arg1,___ARG1,1)\n   205\t___BEGIN_SFUN_ARG(2,___ARG2)\n   223\t___END_SFUN\n   224\treturn ___result;\n   225\t#undef ___NARGS\n   226\t}\n   227\t\n   228\textern int scheme_w3_add_alloc ___P((int ___arg1,int ___arg2),(___arg1,___arg2)\n   229\tint ___arg1;\n   230\tint ___arg2;)\n   231\t{\n   232\t#define ___NARGS 2\n   233\t___BEGIN_SFUN(___MLBL(___C_LBL_scheme_w3_add_alloc),int ___result)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
